= Correlation — Simple =

'''Correlation''' means that two variables change together in some statistical way.

If one usually increases when the other increases, they can have positive correlation.

If one usually increases when the other decreases, they can have negative correlation.

A correlation does not automatically mean one variable causes the other.

Both variables might be affected by something else.

Also, a correlation number near zero can miss strong relationships that are curved or otherwise nonlinear.

It is usually useful to look at the data, not only the correlation coefficient.

